  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: cbc795acf92c1fb48bae236e4ba4d39f, type: 3}
  m_Name: Q02_Intrinsic_Heating
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::PNJunctionLab.AssessmentQuestion
  questionId: q02_temp_ni
  topic: 0
  topicTag: 1. Intrinsic Semiconductors
  shortSummaryLabel: Thermal e-/h+ & Eg
  promptText: Germanium (Eg = 0.67 eV, ni ~ 2.4e13 cm^-3) conducts more than Silicon (Eg = 1.12 eV, ni ~ 1.5e10 cm^-3) at 300 K. When pure Si is heated, what occurs?
  options:
  - Conductivity drops like a metal while ni stays frozen at 1.5e10 cm^-3
  - Only free electrons are created so n becomes 100x greater than p
  - Thermal bond breaking raises n = p = ni exponentially, boosting conductivity
  - The bandgap widens from 1.12 eV to 2.0 eV to stop carrier generation
  correctIndex: 2
  explanation: Thermal energy breaks covalent bonds in equal electron-hole pairs (n = p = ni). Because ni(T) rises exponentially with T, conductivity increases strongly.
  recommendedStation: Station 1
